{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1331496-attainment-question","id":1331496,"title":"Attainment question","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-08-18T12:14:10-07:00","id":1331496,"title":"Attainment question","content":"Hello there! I'm running my first Mage the Awakening 2e table top. (A daunting task - these magic rules are so complicated)<br /> <br /> I've been reading the main book and I'm wondering about the following: Do players have to pay for their Attainments or do they get it for free once they purchase an arcanum level?<br /> <br /> Oh! And another question... what do you think is the rationale for taking the limit away for how many effects can be on an individual at a time? (not to be confused with how many effects a Mage can have active themselves based on gnosis) I mean, I guess I've always had it in my head that the more effects you have on a subject (yourself or someone else for example) the more it affects the integrity of your pattern?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25573-kristi","name":"Kristi","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-18T12:41:08-07:00","id":1331500,"title":"","content":"Legacy attainments require development to learn and that is partially represented by the experience requirement.<br /> <br /> <br /> Attainments from arcana development are automatically all known upon achieving the knowledge of the Arcana, itself.<br /> <br /> <br /> Game rule reasoning: MUCH less housekeeping not to track all the spells present and which count vs. don't.<br /> Narrative reasoning: Not really much of one, one way or the other. Why should a magical effect interfere with your supernal connection? If it does, why couldn't you simply attack a pattern that way with any spells available? In 1e, you absolutely <i>could</i> wreck a target's ability to cast by stacking beneficial spells on them, but the idea has always been that Imago shape the supernal effect brought into the Fallen World.<br /> <br /> If that's going to harm them, why should the harm exclusively interfere with their.ability to cast magic and not bleed into their pattern or require reach to sort out any effect cast on them without invoking a Paradox?<br /> <br /> Ultimately, it's not required by the model they were going for and it complicated things, I figure is the answer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Grump, grouse, and/or gripe.","edited":{"userid":672,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/672-thenate","user":"thenate","date":". 08-18-2019, 03:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/672-thenate","name":"thenate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar672_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}